 to  that growth and is 100 narratively and  emotionally fulfilling to see as he puts  down Cell Max using it it highlights the  growth Gohan has made since cell all the  while undercutting the suspected  distance forming between Piccolo and  Gohan established earlier on as nothing  more than Gohan Having learned his  lesson now training alone off screen  without the need for Piccolo to  constantly remind him furthermore  musically the moment is stunning as  Gohan is achieving this ultimate beastly  form and the action has built up to an  incredible Peak you would expect some  high energy battle theme to be playing  for the transformation and final blow  but Sato rather chooses to completely  subvert that expectation and play to the  quiet Rage of the form the entire  climactic sequence bathed in rays of  purple and greens from the fighters is  accompanied with an immaculately  understated atmospheric piece of music  no huge horn sections blasting away as a  key blast is charged just soft strings  and synth holding on a single note as a  quiet guitar gently Whispers In and Out  of existence once in a while of course  there will be nothing wrong with a more  traditionally climactic track for this  moment but I think we can all agree that  this more subversive approach helps  cement this moment in the minds of every  single viewer as something truly special  furthermore actively achieving what I  saw this film setting up magenta  manipulates Dr hedo into being another  brilliant scientist like gero for him to  utilize and Piccolo attempts to  manipulate Gohan into being who he wants  him to be however both soon realize how  wrong their initial impressions of these  two were later in the film with hero  being far more virtuous than his late  grandfather and Gohan having indeed  continued to grow and train without the  spotlight and watchful eye of his  one-time mentor and master that's a  great structure with appropriately  contrasting forces of Good and Evil in  the mix only the film doesn't really let  that be the case at least not entirely  instead of becoming the sort of person  he needed to be during the Cell Games  we're reminded that Gohan frustratingly  is still very much that individual who  can seem to be of meaningful value to  the team that can't be the game changer  he could be without first emotionally  breaking to remind him of what he's  truly fighting for yet another good  Android Bites the Dust Piccolo on the  edge of death and perhaps in that same  vein following this negative threat  perhaps I'm being too charitable in  categorizing Gohan as someone mindful of  what might await in the future he  mentions later in the film that he's  been keeping up with his training but I  ask why earlier in the film when Piccolo  tells him to keep up his training Gohan  asks why and wonders if something bad  could actually happen to the Earth again  he actually wonders this this is such an  offensively stupid thing for this  character to say I personally think if  there's any character that should know  how turbulent the world can be if  there's anyone that should understand  how suddenly everything can go to hell  it should be Gohan it's been his entire  life and so while I can acknowledge what  the film is trying to say with Gohan's  character I find myself walking a  tightrope where the margin of error is  so easily trespassed into am I being too  harsh or too charitable if I've made  anything clear during this analysis of  Gohan's big moment of the film it's that  I feel extremely conflicted in one  breath relieved and thrilled they seem  to be progressing the character and in  another utterly frustrated and deflated  at the thought of this being another  moment exactly like what existed before  awkwardly inserted for the sake of  saying hey remember that however it is  important to highlight that during this  transformation Gohan feels distinctly  different with the arrogance he once  postured as a child no longer present  however it does still feel at odds to me  the Beast Gohan character aesthetic and  visual hearkens back to imagery from his  character's greatest moments up until  now Gohan's appearance as a child during  the cell game stood in stark contrast  with the rest of his character's journey  and Visually that made sense to me I  liked how the Buu saga wanted to give  him his own identity free from the  shackles of a Super Saiyan's  expectations a sentiment which was  echoed in Super's manga fittingly and I  loved the narrative Direction Super's  anime took the character highlighting  his arrogance against cell and boo  specifically forcing him to become the  man he was always meant to be and while  I love the use of the mcconko sapo by  Gohan in this film with it being easily  one of the best single moments his  character has contributed to since cell  the fact that it's framed in this  strangely and frankly dated  interpretation of the character  aesthetically feels at odds with what  the moment represents to me personally  the giant spiked hair so familiar and  fitting to the arrogance of a young boy  back then seems to have returned and  perhaps appropriately the crown now  doesn't seem to sit comfortably on the  head of this young father anymore almost  like it's forcing an appearance upon  this man of which he's personally  outgrown however alternatively one could  look at this familiar and lofty shape as  his reclaiming of who he once was at the  height of his character's popularity  while the color shift away from the  golden spectacle he boasted during the  cell Saga might also indicate this  noticeable sense of responsibility he  retains while in this form that replaces  the arrogance he embodied during his  younger years once he did Cut Loose  maybe this form is a perfect marriage  between the states of being the Gohan  character has explored opening him up to  embracing what makes who he is as a  character different now maybe maybe I'm  being too cynical maybe I'm being too  charitable or maybe Akira Toriyama just  thought it looked cool whatever the case  you can decide how you feel for  yourselves in my opinion the climax  didn't need Gohan to transform the way  that he did because he had already  transformed in another more meaningful  way that actually mattered in the past  transformations in Dragon Ball signified  a change in mentality it grows beyond  who they once were freeing them to  explode past their latent potential and  do wondrous things as a result result in  this new form the new form didn't bring  about a new personality this new outlook  brought about that new form how they  looked aesthetically while important for  selling toys and admittedly bonding us  to the character was sort of secondary  with the story itself narratively  interweaving the physical changes with  the emotional and spiritual change that  would occur within the character itself  unlike these past occurrences however  despite Dragon Ball super superhero  brandishing a shiny new transformation  for the character that has undergone  personal growth the form and the growth  integral for the transformation to work  in my mind feel entirely disconnected  from each other the reason why Gohan's  Super Saiyan 2 transformation worked so  powerfully was because it was not just  built up to but represented an instance  in time where the character changed both  emotionally and physically together in  this film the emotion and physical  changes feel entirely disconnected from  one another a character having achieved  a sense of personal growth worthy of  praise and celebration off-screen then  undercut by a transformation that feels  like it's scratching and clawing to  recapture what it once was despite the  character now having outgrown it and I  don't think that's fair fair for this  film or fair for it to be a person's  parting feelings concerning it there  were plenty of criticisms I offered in  good faith towards this film but that  isn't to suggest I disliked it at all  because as I mentioned at the beginning  of this video this is almost a perfect  Dragon Ball film to me it's filled with  so much character passion and lore that  upon watching it for my second and  eventually third times around I only  fell in love with it all the more this  film I don't think was ever designed to  shake things up or present the dragon  ball cannon with anything Monumental and  I think that's okay Transformations  aside I think this film had a lot of  heart and at the end of the day a single  moment in time that I didn't enjoy isn't  enough to bring down a film like this  for me because once again this is a  movie about preserving what was through  sacrifice through heroism whether it be  through gamma's sacrifice or whether it  be through this film's touching ending  pan eagerly awaiting to demonstrate her  Newfound ability to soar High into the  air if there's anything I've learned  this year it's that what we have right  now can be taken away at any moment but  similarly to this film's Final Moments  we should always be able to find solace  in the fact that no matter how bad  things get time always Zan the young  progressed forward and there will always  be something new around the next corner  [Music]  I hope I've perhaps encouraged some of  you out there who might have written off  this film entirely to at least give it  another shot it starts slower than I'd  like personally but once it does get  going it's a very fun ride hopefully  Dragon Ball can carry this momentum  onwards to whatever their next  production will be but until then I hope  you enjoyed this video and thank you so  much for the love and support this year  happy Christmas happy holidays and a  very Happy New Year to one and all oh  also this film has a post-credits scene  and after all the praise I've given this  movie for being faithful to the genre  that might just be the most superhero  movie thing this film has done yet  [Music]  it's over  go home
 

